  <div>
    <div data-motion="lesson-classic" data-wf--lesson-classic--variant="delay" class="lesson-classic">
      <div class="lesson-classic-mask">
        <div aria-hidden="true" class="lesson-classic-film w-variant-6e0b570a-f804-b832-c7ae-16cc31f718d8 is-left"></div>
        <div class="lesson-classic-body w-variant-6e0b570a-f804-b832-c7ae-16cc31f718d8">
          <div class="lesson-classic-head">
            <div class="lesson-classic-title"><h3 class="heading-lg">An example from classic animation</h3></div>
            <div class="lesson-classic-list">
              <div class="lesson-classic-media">
                <video
                  data-motion="lesson-video"
                  data-src="https://cdn.zajno.com/dev/motion/videos/Lessons/lesson-2/1_FLOWER_FULL_DEMO_H.264.mp4"
                  muted=""
                  loop=""
                  playsinline="playsinline"
                  preload="none"
                  aria-hidden="true"
                  class="lesson-classic-video"
                ></video
                ><img src="/assets/1_FLOWER_FULL_DEMO_00000-1-.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" class="lesson-classic-poster" />
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="lesson-classic-steps w-variant-6e0b570a-f804-b832-c7ae-16cc31f718d8">
            <div class="lesson-classic-sticky">
              <div class="lesson-classic-step">
                <div class="lesson-classic-text">
                  <p class="body-sm is-strong">Follow through and overlapping action</p>
                  <p class="body-sm">
                    When objects come to a standstill after being in motion, different parts will stop at different speed. Similarly, not everything on an
                    object will move at the same speed. This forms the essence of the fifth of Disney&#x27;s principles of animation.<br /><br />If your
                    character is running across the scene, their arms and legs may be moving at a different speed from their head. This is an overlapping
                    action. Likewise, when they stop running, their hair will likely continue to move for a few frames before coming to rest – this is
                    follow-through. These are essential principles to understand if you want your animation to flow realistically.
                  </p>
                </div>
                <a
                  href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4OxphYV8W3E&amp;ab_channel=AlanBeckerTutorials"
                  target="_blank"
                  class="lesson-classic-link w-inline-block"
                  ><div>View</div>
                  <div class="lesson-classic-icon w-embed">
